Empty catch blocks silently swallow errors from presence and activity API calls. When these calls fail, there's no logging or visibility for debugging. Consider logging errors at minimum, even if graceful fallback to empty arrays is intentional.
```suggestion
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Failed to fetch presence and activity data in AppHeader', error);
```

Optimistic UI update without rollback on error: the comment is added to local state before the API call completes. If the API call fails (empty catch on line 71), the comment remains visible in the UI but doesn't exist on the backend, creating inconsistency. Consider implementing rollback logic on error or showing the comment in a "pending" state until persisted.
```suggestion
        dataSource
          .create('sys_comment', { ...newComment, threadId })
          .catch(() => {
            // Roll back optimistic update if persistence fails
            setComments(prev => prev.filter(comment => comment.id !== newComment.id));
          });
```

Empty catch block silently swallows errors when view save fails. The user sees a generic error toast but developers have no logging for debugging. Consider logging the error with details before showing the toast.
```suggestion
      } catch (error) {
        console.error('Failed to save view configuration', {
          error,
          objectName: objectDef?.name,
          viewId,
          viewLabel: config.viewLabel,
        });
```

Missing test coverage for the update operation path. The tests only verify dataSource.create is called for new views, but there's no test verifying that dataSource.update is called when saving an existing view (when viewId is not 'new'). Consider adding a test for the update scenario.
```suggestion
  });

  it('should call dataSource.update on save for existing view', async () => {
    render(
      <MemoryRouter initialEntries={['/contact/views/v-1']}>
        <Routes>
          <Route path=":objectName/views/:viewId" element={<ViewDesignerPage objects={mockObjects} />} />
        </Routes>
      </MemoryRouter>
    );

    expect(capturedOnSave).toBeTruthy();
    // Simulate save for an existing view
    capturedOnSave!({ viewLabel: 'Updated Contacts', viewType: 'grid', columns: [] });

    await waitFor(() => {
      expect(mockUpdate).toHaveBeenCalled();
    });
  });
```

The save operation continues with navigation even when dataSource is null or the API call fails. If dataSource is null (line 55), no save occurs but the success toast is still shown and navigation proceeds, misleading the user. Similarly, if the API call fails (line 68-70), navigation still happens after the error toast. Consider returning early or conditionally navigating only on success.
```suggestion
      if (!dataSource) {
        toast.error('No data source available to save view configuration');
        return;
      }

      try {
        const payload = {
          objectName: objectDef?.name,
          viewId: viewId === 'new' ? undefined : viewId,
          ...config,
        };

        if (existingView) {
          await dataSource.update('sys_view', viewId!, payload);
        } else {
          await dataSource.create('sys_view', payload);
        }

        toast.success(
          existingView
            ? `View "${config.viewLabel}" updated`
            : `View "${config.viewLabel}" created`,
        );
        navigate(-1);
      } catch {
        toast.error('Failed to save view configuration');
      }
```

